---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = "LK-150 Stitch Patterning (Test) | Knit by Machine";
const description = "Test page for LK-150 tuck stitch patterning foundation section layout.";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-8 sm:py-12">
    
    <!-- Back link (top) -->
    <a href="/" class="inline-flex items-center text-sm text-neutral-600 hover:text-neutral-900 mb-6" data-testid="link-back-top">
      ← Back to home
    </a>

    <!-- Test notice box -->
    <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-lg p-4 mb-8" data-testid="notice-test-page">
      <p class="text-amber-800 text-sm font-medium">This is a test page for previewing the Foundation section layout.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Page title -->
    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold text-neutral-900 mb-2" data-testid="text-page-title">
      LK-150 Stitch Patterning (Test)
    </h1>
    
    <!-- Subtitle -->
    <p class="text-xl text-neutral-600 mb-10" data-testid="text-subtitle">
      Tuck stitches: the mechanics
    </p>

    <!-- Section 1: In one sentence intro -->
    <section class="mb-10">
      <p class="text-lg text-neutral-700 leading-relaxed" data-testid="text-intro-paragraph">
        A tuck stitch is a stitch that doesn't knit—yarn is laid over the needle and builds up for several rows, creating raised bundles and texture when those bundles are finally knitted.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 2: What tuck changes in your fabric -->
    <section class="border rounded-2xl p-6 mb-10" data-testid="card-what-tuck-changes">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 mb-4">What tuck changes in your fabric</h2>
      
      <p class="text-neutral-700 mb-4">
        When you tuck on the same needles for multiple rows, loops gather on those needles instead of forming stitches row-by-row. That buildup distorts plain stockinette in a controlled way.
      </p>
      
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-neutral-700 space-y-1 mb-6">
        <li>raised texture (bundles)</li>
        <li>thicker fabric</li>
        <li>more structure than plain knitting</li>
        <li>the look changes dramatically based on needle arrangement and row counts</li>
      </ul>
      
      <!-- Callout box -->
      <div class="bg-neutral-50 rounded-xl p-4 border-l-4 border-neutral-300" data-testid="callout-why-tuck-magical">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-neutral-800 mb-1">Why tuck feels 'magical'</p>
        <p class="text-neutral-700 text-sm">
          The fun comes from alternating needle arrangements with plain knitting—plus options like color changes and tucking with needles out of work.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 3: Two-image grid -->
    <section class="mb-10">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
        <!-- Placeholder 1 -->
        <div>
          <div class="aspect-[16/10] bg-neutral-200 rounded-xl flex items-center justify-center border-2 border-dashed border-neutral-400" data-testid="placeholder-tuck-drawing">
            <span class="text-neutral-500 text-sm text-center px-4">IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: tuck_drawing.jpg</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-neutral-500 mt-2 text-center">Diagram showing how tuck loops accumulate on the needle</p>
        </div>
        <!-- Placeholder 2 -->
        <div>
          <div class="aspect-[16/10] bg-neutral-200 rounded-xl flex items-center justify-center border-2 border-dashed border-neutral-400" data-testid="placeholder-intro-tuck">
            <span class="text-neutral-500 text-sm text-center px-4">IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: intro_tuck.jpg</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-neutral-500 mt-2 text-center">Example of tuck stitch texture in finished fabric</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 4: LK-150 setup note -->
    <section class="border rounded-2xl p-6 mb-10" data-testid="card-lk150-setup">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 mb-4">LK-150 setup note: selecting needles</h2>
      
      <p class="text-neutral-700 mb-4">
        On the LK-150, you create tuck by pulling needles to Hold position (D) for the needles you want to tuck.
      </p>
      
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-neutral-700 space-y-1 mb-6">
        <li>Needle pushers</li>
        <li>A small mark on the needle bed (short repeats)</li>
        <li>A cheat sheet/grid (best when you don't want to count)</li>
      </ul>
      
      <!-- Callout box -->
      <div class="bg-neutral-50 rounded-xl p-4 border-l-4 border-neutral-300" data-testid="callout-keep-simple">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-neutral-800 mb-1">Keep it simple at first</p>
        <p class="text-neutral-700 text-sm">
          Start with a repeat you can see without counting. Once the mechanics make sense, complexity becomes optional.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 5: Reading tuck charts -->
    <section class="border rounded-2xl p-6 mb-10" data-testid="card-reading-tuck-charts">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 mb-4">Reading tuck charts (and punchcards)</h2>
      
      <p class="text-neutral-700 mb-6">
        Understanding how to read a tuck chart helps you translate any pattern—whether it's hand-drawn, from a book, or adapted from a punchcard design.
      </p>
      
      <!-- Two placeholder boxes side by side -->
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mb-6">
        <div>
          <div class="aspect-[4/3] bg-neutral-200 rounded-xl flex items-center justify-center border-2 border-dashed border-neutral-400" data-testid="placeholder-tuck-symbol">
            <span class="text-neutral-500 text-sm text-center px-4">IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: tuck_symbol.jpg</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-neutral-500 mt-2 text-center">Common tuck symbol notation</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="aspect-[4/3] bg-neutral-200 rounded-xl flex items-center justify-center border-2 border-dashed border-neutral-400" data-testid="placeholder-tucked-blocks">
            <span class="text-neutral-500 text-sm text-center px-4">IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: tucked_blocks_pattern.jpg</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-neutral-500 mt-2 text-center">Example tuck block pattern chart</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      
      <!-- Callout box -->
      <div class="bg-neutral-50 rounded-xl p-4 border-l-4 border-neutral-300 mb-6" data-testid="callout-punchcard-designs">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-neutral-800 mb-1">Using punchcard designs (LK-150 friendly)</p>
        <p class="text-neutral-700 text-sm">
          You can borrow punchcard designs as pattern inspiration. A tuck punchcard is typically mostly holes. When hand-selecting needles, pull needles to correspond with the non-hole (white) spaces.
        </p>
      </div>
      
      <!-- One more placeholder -->
      <div class="max-w-sm mx-auto">
        <div class="aspect-[4/3] bg-neutral-200 rounded-xl flex items-center justify-center border-2 border-dashed border-neutral-400" data-testid="placeholder-good-punchcard">
          <span class="text-neutral-500 text-sm text-center px-4">IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: good_punchcard.bmp</span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-neutral-500 mt-2 text-center">Example of an LK-150-friendly punchcard pattern</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 6: Quick self-check -->
    <section class="border rounded-2xl p-6 mb-10" data-testid="card-self-check">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 mb-4">Quick self-check</h2>
      
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-neutral-700 space-y-2">
        <li>Which needles are tucking (held) vs knitting?</li>
        <li>What happens when you tuck over the same needle for multiple rows?</li>
        <li>Why do needle arrangements matter more than the chart "look"?</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Back link (bottom) -->
    <a href="/" class="inline-flex items-center text-sm text-neutral-600 hover:text-neutral-900 mt-4" data-testid="link-back-bottom">
      ← Back to home
    </a>

  </div>
</BaseLayout>
